<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="bases-complete">
    <title>Bases (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We have some choice in how we identify vectors in a vector space, and in many cases, some choices 
                        are better than others. Choosing how to represent vectors is equivalent to choosing a 
                        <term>basis</term> for the vector space.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Bases in <m>\R^2</m></title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                When we write <m>\langle 1, 2 \rangle</m> for a vector in <m>\R^2</m>, there is little confusion.
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        These coordinates identify where to move in the horizontal and vertical directions, respectively. But
                        we are utilizing a specific basis of <m>\R^2</m> to interpret the coordinates:
                        <md>
                            \langle 1, 2\rangle = 1\mathbf{i} + 2\mathbf{j} = 1\langle 1,0\rangle + 2 \langle 0,1\rangle
                        </md>
                        <m>\beta = \{\langle 1,0\rangle, \langle 0,1\rangle \}</m> is known as the <term>standard basis</term> 
                        for <m>\R^2</m>, and <m>\langle 1, 2 \rangle</m> provides the scalar weights to be used on each basis 
                        vector (in order) to specify our vector.
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        There are many bases of <m>\R^2</m>! Another one is <m>\mathscr{C} = \{\langle 1, 1\rangle, \langle -1, 1\rangle \}</m>.
                        In the <m>\mathscr{C}</m>-basis, <m>\langle 1,2\rangle</m> means
                        <md>
                            1\cdot \langle 1,1\rangle + 2 \cdot \langle -1,1\rangle = \langle -1, 3\rangle
                        </md>
                        in standard coordinates. 
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>General Bases</title>
             
                <statement>
                    <p>
                      A set of vectors <m> \beta = \{\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \dots , \vvec_n\}</m> is a <term>basis</term> 
                      for a vector space <m>V</m> if 
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\beta</m> is a <term>linearly independent</term> set of vectors. This means that 
                                    a linear combination of the vectors is <m>\mathbf{0}</m> exactly when all scaling 
                                    factors are <m>0</m>.
                                </p>
                                <p>
                                    This results in all vectors having unique coordinates with respect to the basis.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>\beta</m> <term>spans</term> all of <m>V</m>. This means every vector in <m>V</m>
                                    can be written as a linear combination of basis vectors.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Examples: Bases</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
         The number of vectors in a basis defined the <term>dimension</term> of a vector space.
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Some examples of vector spaces and a basis (and thus the dimension) are
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\R^2</m> over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{\langle 1,0 \rangle, \langle 0,1 \rangle \}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\R^2) = 2</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\R^n</m> over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{\mathbf{e}_1, \dots, \mathbf{e_n} \}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\R^n) = n</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\C</m> over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{1,i \}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\C) = 2</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\C^n</m> over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{\mathbf{e}_1,i\mathbf{e}_1, \dots, \mathbf{e_n}, i\mathbf{e_n} \}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\C^n) = 2n</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\C^n</m> over <m>\C</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{\mathbf{e}_1, \dots, \mathbf{e_n}\}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\C^n) = n</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                     <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\mathbb{P}^n</m> over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{1, x, \dots, x^n\}</m>, 
                            <m>\text{dim}(\mathbb{P}^n) = n+1</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                     <li>
                        <p>
                            All polynomials over <m>\R</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{1, x, \dots, x^n, \dots\}</m>, 
                            infinite dimensional
                        </p>
                    </li>
                     <li>
                        <p>
                            Hilbert space of wave functions over <m>\C</m>, 
                            <m>\beta = \{\psi_1, \psi_2, \dots, \psi_n, \dots\}</m>, 
                            infinite dimensional
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Choosing Bases</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
          Not all bases are created equal for your application! You may wish to choose a basis based on 
        </p>
       </statement> 
       <solution>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Basis properties (eg., orthogonal, normal)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Physics (eg., position space, momentum space)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            We'll see the basics for changing bases soon once we have some more linear algebra tools.
        </p>
       </solution>
       
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
